  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...All eyes on Tropical Storm Isaias.\n\nQuiet weather will continue for the rest of the work week with a \ncold front passing through Thursday to deliver additional dry and \ncool temperatures. The main focus of the forecast shifts to Tropical \nStorm Isaias which is expected to become a hurricane within the\nnext 24 hours. Model guidance, both physical and AI based, are \nin agreement with this system making landfall (exact locations\ndiffer) and heading north into the Deep South. Global models \nsuggest that Isaias will weaken into a Post-Tropical Storm \nDepression by this weekend but may veer sharply eastward towards\nthe Mid-Atlantic. This is supported by the mid-level weather \npattern which has a jet over the CONUS/Canadian border. By \nSaturday night, Isaias will become more of a trough and get \ncaught in the westerly flow which swings it towards the east \ncoast. This suggests a mult-iday period of showers for our \narea. How much rain we get will depend on the actual path of the\nstorm but early projections have up to 1 to 3 inches over the \nentire event. This is not enough to cause any major flood \nconcerns but some areas, such as the southern Blue Ridge, may \nreceive higher amounts from all the southward moving rain from \nIsaias. The rain from Isaias may also start as early as Saturday\nnight and may continue as late as Tuesday morning. The remnants\nof Isaias will still have a relatively strong pressure gradient\nwhich may deliver some gusty winds up to 35 mph as it goes \nthrough. Following the previously mentioned cold front, a strong\nhigh pressure system centered over New England will be \nentrenched into the mountains prior to Isaias's arrival. The end\nresult will be plenty of showers but little to no convection \nand the risk of thunderstorms, let alone severe thunderstorms, \nis low. This forecast will evolve as Tropical Storm Isaias \nstrengthens and additional data comes in.",
